-- EXPECT-ERROR: division by `n` in `cap no_panic` module may be by zero
-- Refinement types, DECL-WALK COVERAGE (2026-07-29). `cap no_panic` promises
-- a module's divisions cannot panic. Until this file existed, the walk that
-- raises division obligations (lib/refinecheck/division_safety.ml) descended
-- only into `DFn` and `DMod` and ended in a `| _ -> ()` wildcard, so a
-- division living in ANY other declaration form — an `impl` method, a
-- top-level `let`, an actor handler, a `test` body — was never looked at.
-- This exact program therefore passed `--check` with exit 0 and then died at
-- run time with "division by zero": the capability's central promise, silently
-- unenforced for every method body in the module.
--
-- The identical division written inside a plain `fn` was always rejected, so
-- this was coverage, not solver weakness. Both walks are now exhaustive over
-- `A.decl` with no wildcard — a 25th decl form is a compile error rather than
-- a new silent hole.
--
-- Accept companion: accept/t119, the same impl body with an `if n != 0` guard,
-- which the widened walk approves. Read the pair together: this file shows the
-- impl body IS being read, its companion shows reading it does not false-
-- positive on a guarded divisor.
mod ImplDiv do
  needs IO.Console
  cap no_panic

  type Box = Box(Int)

  interface Runner(a) do
    fn run : a -> Int
  end

  impl Runner(Box) do
    fn run(b) do
      match b do
        Box(n) -> 100 / n
      end
    end
  end

  fn main() do
    println(int_to_string(run(Box(2))))
  end
end
